he W6 might be better for potential Pung. \n\nTherefore, the best tile to discard is B4. Because it's a single tile that's not part of any potential melds, and discarding it allows the user to focus on forming a Pung of B7s or a Chow with B4-B5-B6 if they keep it. Wait, but they are discarding B4, so they can't use it for a Chow. So if they discard B4, they lose the chance to form a Chow of B4-B5-B6. \n\nHmm. So there's a trade-off. If they keep B4, they can form a Chow if they draw B5. If they discard B4, they can focus on B7s. \n\nWhich is more likely? B5 has 3 left, B7 has 4 left. So B7 is more likely to be drawn. So discarding B4 might be better. \n\nTherefore, the best action is to play B4.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [The hand needs two more melds and a pair. Discarding B4 allows focusing on forming a Pung of B7s or a Chow of B7-B8-B9. B4 is not part of any potential melds and discarding it reduces risk.]\nReason: [B4 is a single tile with no immediate use in current hand. Discarding it preserves B7 pair for potential Pung and avoids giving others a needed tile. B7 has 4 remaining in wall, increasing chance to complete a Pung.]\nAnswer: Play B4"}
